			"widgets_values":[
				"integrated_multimodal_description:\n\n[Shot 1] A single continuous 12-second cinematic fantasy-realism shot inside a beautiful antique observatory workshop at blue hour.\n\nThe scene begins with a close-up of a young adult female watchmaker sitting at a dark wooden workbench. She is elegant, intelligent-looking, and naturally beautiful, around her mid-20s, with neat dark hair tied loosely back, clear skin, expressive eyes, and a calm, focused expression. She wears a simple cream blouse with rolled sleeves, a charcoal vest, and understated brass accessories. Her overall appearance feels refined, realistic, and timeless.\n\nThe observatory workshop is filled with intricate brass gears, pocket watches, celestial charts, glass instruments, old books, and a large mechanical orrery in the background. Warm practical lamp light mixes with cool moon-blue light from a circular glass ceiling above, creating a rich and atmospheric contrast. Tiny floating dust particles catch the light. The whole space feels realistic, tactile, and full of detail.\n\nAt 0–3 seconds, the camera starts in a macro close-up on her hands as she gently places the final tiny brass gear into an open pocket watch. Her fingers move carefully and naturally. The gear clicks into place with precision. The polished metal surfaces reflect the surrounding warm light.\n\nAt 3–5 seconds, the watch begins to tick. The camera slowly pulls back from the macro detail into a medium shot, revealing more of the workshop. As the ticking grows clearer, subtle golden ripples of light travel outward from the pocket watch across the workbench. Nearby small gears begin to rotate on their own. Papers flutter slightly. The woman looks up with quiet wonder.\n\nAt 5–8 seconds, the camera continues moving backward and slightly upward in one uninterrupted motion. The large orrery in the background comes alive, slowly turning with elegant mechanical precision. Constellation lines softly illuminate in the air above the room, as if invisible star charts are being drawn in real time. The workshop begins to feel larger and more magical, but still physically believable. The woman stands up naturally, holding the pocket watch in one hand, and takes a few slow steps forward while looking upward.\n\nAt 8–10 seconds, the circular glass ceiling above gradually opens like a mechanical iris. Moonlight pours in, and the space above transforms into a breathtaking night sky filled with visible constellations and a luminous band of stars. Several tiny brass birds, assembled from fragments of gears and clock hands, lift gently from the workbench and fly upward around her in a graceful spiral. Their motion is elegant and believable, not chaotic.\n\nAt 10–12 seconds, the camera settles into a wider medium-wide shot, framing the woman beneath the open dome. She raises the pocket watch slightly and softly speaks one short poetic line while gazing at the glowing constellations. Her expression is calm, amazed, and quietly emotional:\n\n<d>[Chinese] 有时候，时间不是在流逝，而是在发光。</d>\n\nHer Mandarin voice is soft, clear, intimate, and natural, with subtle breath and realistic lip sync. The entire clip must remain photorealistic, emotionally rich, and visually coherent. Motion must be smooth and continuous. The woman’s facial identity, hairstyle, clothing, proportions, and voice must remain completely consistent throughout the shot.\n\nPrioritize realism in all materials and motion: believable metal reflections, realistic glass, natural hand articulation, subtle fabric movement, physically plausible lighting, graceful camera motion, and stable facial details. The atmosphere should feel like a premium cinematic short film, not a game cutscene and not an AI-generated surreal collage.\n\nKeep the whole sequence as one continuous shot with no cuts.\n\nDo not include subtitles, captions, logos, watermark, extra characters, deformed fingers, floating objects without motivation, unstable face changes, lip-sync drift, flickering background, or exaggerated visual effects.\n\noverall_soundscape:\n\nDelicate clock ticking is the key sound motif throughout the clip. Soft metallic clicks, tiny gear rotations, light paper rustling, subtle cloth movement, faint room ambience, and a gentle night breeze entering from above. As the observatory awakens, add refined mechanical whirs and a soft airy celestial resonance. The woman’s dialogue remains clear and intimate, never overwhelmed by ambient sound.\n\nnon_diegetic_music:\n\nA very subtle, elegant cinematic score with soft piano, warm strings, and delicate glassy textures. The music begins minimal and intimate, then gently expands as the observatory transforms, creating a sense of wonder and emotional lift without overpowering the scene.",
